course: dictionary
lesson: 10148
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is right. The word throttle is a good example. «Pilots fly with a stick or yoke, pedals and throttles, and read screens showing speed, height and direction.» The words pilots, pedals and screens tell you this is about machines, so a throttle is a control that changes how much power goes to an engine.
explain: Throttle can also be a verb, meaning to squeeze someone's throat so they cannot breathe. This is very dangerous and a serious crime. It can also mean to slow or limit something, such as a connection speed. Ask yourself: is the thing being throttled a person or a machine? That question alone clears up most of the confusion.
explain: Sometimes people say things they do not mean. «I could throttle him!» means you are very cross with him. It does not mean you would really hurt him. To check, swap in a meaning: "I could be very cross with him" still makes sense, so that is the right meaning.
fact: A throttle is a control that changes how much fuel or power goes to an engine.
fact: «Full throttle» means with all your energy, or at top speed.
fact: To throttle someone means to squeeze their throat so they cannot breathe; this is very dangerous and a serious crime.
fact: Never put your hands or anything else around anyone's neck, even as a game.
fact: To throttle something can mean to slow it or limit it, like a connection speed.
exercise: «Pilots fly with a stick or yoke, pedals and throttles.» In this sentence, what is a throttle? | A person who flies planes | A type of cloud | A kind of bird | *A control that changes how much power goes to the engine
exercise: A driver says, «I'll open the throttle.» What does this mean? | To close the car door | *To give the engine more power so it speeds up | To stop the engine for good | To call for help
exercise: Ben says, «I could throttle him!» What does he mean? | He is going to give him a present | He is very hungry | *He is very cross with him | He is going to squeeze his throat
test: Someone says it is only a game to put their hands around your neck. What should you do? | *Say no and tell a grown-up | Ask for a bigger game | Join in, because it is only a game | Hold your breath for them
test: «The company decided to throttle the internet speed during the day.» What does throttle mean here? | To speed up hugely | To switch off for ever | *To slow or limit | To squeeze tightly
exam: Which phrase means doing something with all your energy? | Put on your coat | Count to ten | *Go full throttle | Lose the map
